{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall surely give to them, etc.</b> It should have said לָהֵן — and likewise, 'in the midst of the brothers of their father' — it should have said אֲבִיהֶן, as it says immediately afterward: ״וְהַעֲבַרְתָּ אֶת נַחֲלַת אֲבִיהֶן לָהֶן״. And this is because in the matter of inheritance, when there is no son, they stand in the place of males for everything; therefore it says ״נָתֹן תִּתֵּן לָהֶם״ as one speaking to males, to instruct that they are like males for every matter of inheritance. But afterward, when it says ״וְהַעֲבַרְתָּ״, which indicates the language of passing over, or the language of transfer of inheritance from tribe to tribe on account of their being females, therefore it speaks as one speaking to females: ״וְהַעֲבַרְתָּ נַחֲלַת אֲבִיהֶן לָהֶן״." "And what Rashi explained — that they said: we stand in the place of a son, and if not, let our mother perform yibum — it appears that he derived this precisely from what they said: ״כִּי בְחֶטְאוֹ מֵת וּבָנִים לֹא הָיוּ לוֹ״, which implies: if he had had sons, even though he does not have sons now, nevertheless since he once had sons, after his death they would not have claimed anything at all — and why? Because then the dilemma would have been nullified, for they could not have said, let our mother perform yibum. And for this reason Rashi was precise in his language, explaining ״כִּי אֵין לוֹ בֵן״ — this implies if he had a son — and did not explain, this implies if he has a son." "And its plain sense is thus: since they said ״לָמָּה יִגָּרַע שֵׁם אָבִינוּ״ — but is the inheritance of sons dependent on the perpetuation of their father's name? And furthermore, why did they say first ״וּבָנִים לֹא הָיוּ לוֹ״, which implies that if he had had sons, even though they are not now in the world, nevertheless they would not have said this thing — and afterward they said: ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״, because he has no son, which implies that the matter depends only on the facts at this moment? Rather, this is what they said: whichever way you turn it — if we are not counted as seed, then he never had sons; if so, ״לָמָּה יִגָּרַע שֵׁם אָבִינוּ״, and let our mother perform yibum to establish the name of the deceased upon his inheritance; and if we are counted as seed, if so, ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״, for he has no son now to inherit him, and we are in the place of a son. And what they said, ״כִּי אֵין לוֹ בֵן״, refers to the conclusion, where they said ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״. And this explanation is clear and correct."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "Rashi's reading turns on the past tense לֹא הָיוּ ('were not') vs. present אֵין ('is not'); the Kli Yakar's argument hinges on that distinction throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:23.883613596Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}